  "question": {
    "text": "Let an ellipse <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>:</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#62;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, passes through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> and has eccentricity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>. If a circle, centered at focus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> and radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>, intersects <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> at two points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> is equal to :",
    "image": null
  },
